      {
        "id": "relay-unit",
        "label": "Permit lamp: A/B request, C inhibit",
        "reading": {
          "text": "A and B are two entry requests; C is an inhibit. In this illustrative permit-lamp model, 1 means a condition holds and output Y=1 means the lamp is on. A contact named A closes when A=1; B works likewise. The complementary NOT C contact closes when C=0 and opens when C=1. Treat all contacts as ideal and instantaneous. This is a symbolic model, not wiring instructions for a real door or a safety system.",
          "attribution": "Authored ideal-contact teaching case using the inherited Boolean rule; not historical measurements or construction instructions. Shannon (1938), sections I–II, uses the dual hindrance convention."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "series-layout",
        "label": "Series: both contacts must close"
      },
      {
        "id": "parallel-layout",
        "label": "Parallel: either branch may conduct"
      },
      {
        "id": "reversing-contact",
        "label": "NOT C: closed at C=0, open at C=1"
      },
      {
        "id": "duplicate-layout",
        "label": "Two NOT C contacts: 4-contact layout",
        "reading": {
          "text": "The distributed layout has two parallel branches: one is A in series with NOT C; the other is B in series with another NOT C. Its expression is (A AND NOT C) OR (B AND NOT C). Count four contacts: A, B, and two separate contacts controlled by the same C. The duplication is the inhibit contact, not an independent backup. Compare with one NOT C shared by both request paths. Count contacts only, not relay coils, wires, or total cost.",
          "attribution": "Authored ideal-contact teaching case using the inherited Boolean rule; not historical measurements or construction instructions. Shannon (1938), sections I–II, uses the dual hindrance convention."
        }
      },
