        "id": "not-circuit",
        "label": "NOT C: C=0 → 1; C=1 → 0"
      },
      {
        "id": "specified-relay",
        "label": "Target: Y=(A OR B) AND NOT C",
        "reading": {
          "text": "Bring forward the checked entry rule, (A OR B) AND NOT C. A and B now denote requests and C the inhibit for the illustrative permit lamp. Both requests together still count: OR is inclusive. The final AND requires a request path AND absence of the inhibit. Three independent inputs require eight rows, not the four rows used for the earlier A/B-only operation. Formal correctness does not establish that an entry policy is just.",
          "attribution": "Authored ideal-contact teaching case using the inherited Boolean rule; not historical measurements or construction instructions. Shannon (1938), sections I–II, uses the dual hindrance convention."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "permission-network",
        "label": "Request paths: A OR B; inhibit still needed",
        "reading": {
          "text": "The parallel request contacts implement A OR B. They supply the request part of the inherited rule, not the whole rule: at A=1, B=0, C=1 these request paths conduct, although the final output must be off. NOT C must block both requests, not only B.",
          "attribution": "Authored ideal-contact teaching case using the inherited Boolean rule; not historical measurements or construction instructions. Shannon (1938), sections I–II, uses the dual hindrance convention."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "blocked-entry-network",
        "label": "Both requests inhibited: 4 contacts",
        "reading": {
          "text": "The corrected distributed model is (A AND NOT C) OR (B AND NOT C): A and a complementary C contact form one series branch, B and a second complementary C contact the other. The two branches are parallel. C=1 opens both inhibit contacts, so neither request bypasses the inhibit. At ABC=101 the corrected output is 0, unlike the rival’s 1. At 100 it is 1. This specifies the ideal contact behavior, not a real installation.",
          "attribution": "Authored ideal-contact teaching case using the inherited Boolean rule; not historical measurements or construction instructions. Shannon (1938), sections I–II, uses the dual hindrance convention."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "tested-relay-network",
        "label": "Eight rows checked; 101 and 111 corrected",
        "reading": {
          "text": "ABC → Y for the inherited rule and the corrected four-contact model: 000→0; 001→0; 010→1; 011→0; 100→1; 101→0; 110→1; 111→0. Thus either request alone or both together works when C=0; every C=1 row is off. The rival A OR (B AND NOT C) instead gives 1 at 101 and 111. These are all eight static input cases of this model, not tests of timing, broken contacts, or real hardware.",
          "attribution": "Authored ideal-contact teaching case using the inherited Boolean rule; not historical measurements or construction instructions. Shannon (1938), sections I–II, uses the dual hindrance convention."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "equivalent-circuit",
        "label": "Same 8 outputs; 4 → 3 contacts",
        "reading": {
          "text": "Factor the repeated inhibit: (A AND NOT C) OR (B AND NOT C) = (A OR B) AND NOT C. The factored model has A and B in parallel, with one shared NOT C contact in series with that pair: three contacts rather than four, saving one. For ABC=000,001,010,011,100,101,110,111, both models give Y=0,0,1,0,1,0,1,0. Equivalence means the same eight ideal, static outputs. It does not imply equal fault tolerance: if the shared NOT C sticks closed, C no longer inhibits either request; a stuck-closed C contact in the four-contact model bypasses the inhibit on its own branch. No reliability, timing, or total-cost claim follows from the contact saving.",
          "attribution": "Authored ideal-contact teaching case using the inherited Boolean rule; not historical measurements or construction instructions. Shannon (1938), sections I–II, uses the dual hindrance convention."
        }
